            "privateNotes": "",
            "publicNotes": "<p>The bannik is the spirit of the bathhouse—also called the sauna or banya—and resembles a wizened little person with wild hair and nails of pine or fir. While domovois consider themselves to be in charge of a home, banniks are the wisest and most respected of the household spirits. Traditionally, the third steaming of the bath belongs to the bannik and their guests (often other faerie creatures), and to interrupt it is a deadly danger.</p><hr /><p>House spirits are a collection of fey who live among peasants and poor rural folk. They hide themselves from mortal eyes—hence why they're so commonly called \"spirits\"—but their presence is always felt. Given suitable respect, these house fey take a decidedly parental interest in the mortals they live with, doing all manner of work around the household. They cook, they clean, they sweep, they chop wood, they care for animals, they tend the stove. If disrespected, however, they becomes a veritable plague of ill will.</p>",
            "publication": {
                "license": "ORC",
                "remaster": true,
                "title": "Pathfinder Feybound"
            }
